<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Remaining keys are generated from the INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings
	  (GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays on and merges into this file). This file holds the
	  keys that cannot be expressed as a build setting, or that are easier to read with
	  their reasoning beside them.
	-->

	<!--
	  Export compliance. Without this key App Store Connect asks the export questions on
	  every upload and holds the build at "Missing Compliance" until they are answered,
	  which blocks repeated TestFlight builds.

	  The app implements no cryptography of its own and ships none: data at rest uses
	  CryptoKit AES-GCM, and every TLS connection goes through Apple's stack. Checked
	  2026-11-01 against the device build: swift-nio-ssl (which vendors BoringSSL) is in
	  Package.resolved but is not linked into the app; swift-crypto re-exports CryptoKit
	  on Apple platforms; and aws-crt-swift uses CommonCrypto and Network.framework TLS
	  on iOS (s2n and AWS-LC are its Linux backends). The owner signed off on `false` the
	  same day. Re-check if a dependency that brings its own TLS or crypto is added:
	  `nm HealthApp.debug.dylib | grep -E "CNIOBoringSSL|CCryptoBoringSSL|aws_lc_|s2n_"`
	  should print nothing.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<!--
		  From iOS 17 on, ATS blocks cleartext connections to IP addresses by default.
		  Self-hosted OpenAI-compatible inference servers (Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM,
		  LiteLLM) normally run on the LAN over plain HTTP with no certificate, so
		  without this the local-server feature cannot connect at all.

		  This is the narrow exception: it permits cleartext only to private and
		  link-local IP ranges, .local domains, and unqualified host names. Public
		  hosts still require HTTPS, and OpenAICompatibleEndpointValidator enforces
		  the same rule in the app before a request is ever built.
		-->
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
